/**
 * @page topic_crypto Cryptography and Session Security
 * @ingroup module_crypto
 * @brief Authentication, ephemeral session encryption, and rekeying.
 *
 * ascii-chat separates identity from session confidentiality. SSH/Ed25519,
 * GPG-backed Ed25519, passwords, and pinned public keys authenticate policy;
 * ephemeral key exchange derives the session encryption keys.
 *
 * @section crypto_handshake Handshake
 *
 * The crypto handshake negotiates capabilities and parameters, performs the
 * key exchange, authenticates the peer when policy requires it, and only then
 * permits encrypted application packets. Transport TLS or DTLS authenticates
 * its own endpoint but does not replace ACIP authentication and identity policy.
 *
 * @section crypto_records Records and replay
 *
 * Encrypted records carry an epoch and sequence. The receiver authenticates and
 * checks replay/order state before dispatching the inner ACIP packet. CRC and
 * packet-family validation still apply at the packet layer. Never log passwords,
 * private keys, session keys, or decrypted sensitive payloads.
 *
 * @section crypto_rekey Rekeying
 *
 * Long-lived sessions can negotiate a new epoch. The old and new key state
 * overlap only for the bounded transition; completion is acknowledged before
 * old material is discarded. Rekey failures terminate the secure session rather
 * than continuing with ambiguous key state.
 *
 * @section crypto_identity Identity sources
 *
 * Key loading and validation are documented in @ref topic_keys. ACDS identity
 * keys and server-key pinning are separate from the ephemeral encryption keys.
 * Host-key changes require explicit trust policy; discovery metadata does not
 * override an existing identity decision.
 *
 * @see topic_handshake
 * @see topic_keys
 * @see md_docs_2crypto-policy.html
 */
